Leveraged funds were **more short than the model expected**: the residual of **−3.24% of open interest** is about **169,000 contracts**. If those shorts are directional bets, that tilt is bearish for Treasury futures prices and could put upward pressure on yields.

But the positioning alone doesn’t establish that view. Leveraged funds also short Treasury futures as part of cash–futures basis trades, often while holding Treasuries in cash. CFTC positioning data don’t reveal the strategy behind each position. [New York Fed research](https://www.newyorkfed.org/medialibrary/media/research/staff_reports/sr1010.pdf) describes this pairing, and the [CFTC’s notes](https://www.cftc.gov/MarketReports/CommitmentsofTraders/ExplanatoryNotes/index.htm) explain how spreading is reported.

So the snapshot suggests **an unusually strong short tilt relative to this model**, but not necessarily a straightforward bearish market signal. If the short positions are crowded, an unwind could also mean futures buying and a sharp move the other way. The residual is a gap from the model’s expectation, not a price forecast; the figures alone don’t show whether that gap is statistically unusual or what caused it.